cratic fundraising edge and heavy outside spending defending the seat", "Markets price Vasquez around 92% while most raters call it Likely or Lean D", "Even-year midterm environment favors Democrats per fundamentals"], "rationale": "Vasquez holds a modest but consistent polling lead, a large cash advantage, and incumbency in a district with even partisan lean, while markets and most raters favor him clearly. The narrow polls and heavy Republican ad spending argue for trimming the consensus margin to the mid-single digits.", "watch": "A late wave of outside ads against Vasquez, or a polling shift toward Cunningham after the debates, could move this into tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's 2nd district is a genuinely competitive seat with an even partisan lean, but first-term Democrat Gabe Vasquez enters the final stretch with several advantages. He has outraised Republican Greg Cunningham by more than two to one, and the two recent live-caller polls show him ahead by three to four points among likely voters. Outside groups have poured millions into the district, mostly attacking Vasquez, reflecting how contested the race remains.\n\nRatings cluster around Likely D with a few Lean D calls, and prediction markets price Vasquez's chances near 92 percent. The fundamentals, including a favorable midterm environment for his party, point to a wider margin than the polls alone suggest. Cunningham has earned local editorial endorsements and debated vigorously, keeping the race on the board, but Vasquez remains the clear favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8688, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8708, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.87, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8306, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8384, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09